      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The pathogenetic basis for the relationship between acute bronchiolitis and asthma has not yet been completely elucidated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to compare these 2 diseases in terms of their patterns of airway cytokine response (T(H)1 or T(H)2)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "By using a bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) technique, this study investigated the cytokine levels of BAL fluid in children with acute asthma who had no identifiable respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection (n = 18) and in infants with acute bronchiolitis caused by RSV (n = 22). Comparisons were made with normal control subjects (n = 14). IFN-gamma (T(H)1) and IL-4 and IL-5 (T(H)2) levels were measured in concentrated BAL fluids by means of ELISA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The IL-5 level (P <.001) and IL-5/IFN-gamma ratio (P <.001) were significantly increased in the asthmatic group with no identifiable RSV infection and in the RSV-induced bronchiolitis group compared with values in the control group. When infants in the bronchiolitis group were divided into eosinophil-positive and eosinophil-negative subgroups, the eosinophil-positive subgroup had significantly increased IL-5 levels (P <.001) and IL-5/IFN-gamma ratios (P <.01) compared with those in the control group, but similar cytokine responses were not induced in the eosinophil-negative subgroup. The percentage of BAL eosinophils correlated significantly with levels of BAL IL-5 in both the asthma group (r = 0.80, P =.000) and the bronchiolitis group (r = 0.82, P =.000)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These findings suggest that a subgroup of the RSV-induced bronchiolitis group results in a T(H)2-type response, and this could provide a valuable framework to explain the link between RSV-induced bronchiolitis and asthma."
        }
      ]},
